      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's twisting narrative structure belongs to a rich tradition of psychological thrillers that question reality itself. Like <a href=\"/movie/1018\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1018\">Mulholland Drive</a> (2001) and <a href=\"/movie/44214\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"44214\">Black Swan</a> (2010), Shutter Island uses unreliable perspective to create mounting tension and uncertainty. Scorsese's meticulous attention to period detail in the 1950s setting creates an atmosphere of Cold War paranoia that echoes classic noir films like <a href=\"/movie/678\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"678\">Out of the Past</a> (1947)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The asylum setting becomes a character itself, with its Gothic architecture and stormy isolation recalling <a href=\"/movie/10972\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10972\">Session 9</a> (2001) and <a href=\"/movie/207933\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"207933\">Stonehearst Asylum</a> (2014). Scorsese uses this environment to explore themes of institutional power and control similar to <a href=\"/movie/340837\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"340837\">A Cure for Wellness</a> (2016), while the psychological manipulation aspects connect to <a href=\"/movie/234\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"234\">The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari</a> (1920). SUBHEAD: Psychological Reality vs Delusion"
      },
